{"name":"Elephant in the Room","hex":"#a8a9a8","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"elephant in the room","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A problem or difficult issue that is very obvious, but is ignored for the convenience or comfort of those involved."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/elephant_in_the_room"}],"entries":[{"title":"Elephant in the room","blocks":[{"text":"The expression \"the elephant in the room\" is an English language idiom for an important and obvious topic that is left undiscussed due to its awkward or controversial nature. It is based on the idea and thought that something as conspicuous as an elephant can appear to be overlooked in codified social interactions and that the sociology and psychology of repression also operates on the macro scale."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Elephant%27s_tea_party%2C_Robur_Tea_Room%2C_Sydney%2C_24_March_1939_-_Sam_Hood_%283529604677%29.jpg/330px-Elephant%27s_tea_party%2C_Robur_Tea_Room%2C_Sydney%2C_24_March_1939_-_Sam_Hood_%283529604677%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephant_in_the_room"},{"title":"Elephant in the Room (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Elephant in the Room is the third studio album by American rapper Mick Jenkins, released on October 29, 2021, via Free Nation and Cinematic Music Group."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/e/e5/Mick-elephant-in-the-room.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephant_in_the_Room_(album)"},{"title":"Elephant","blocks":[{"text":"Elephants are the largest living land animals. Three living species are currently recognised: the African bush elephant, the African forest elephant, and the Asian elephant. They are the only surviving members of the family Elephantidae and the order Proboscidea; extinct relatives include mammoths and mastodons. Distinctive features of elephants include a long proboscis called a trunk, tusks, large ear flaps, pillar-like legs, and tough but sensitive grey skin. The trunk is prehensile, bringing food and water to the mouth and grasping objects. Tusks, which are derived from the incisor teeth, serve both as weapons and as tools for moving objects and digging. The large ear flaps assist in maintaining a constant body temperature as well as in communication. African elephants have larger ears and concave backs, whereas Asian elephants have smaller ears and convex or level backs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/African_Bush_Elephant.jpg/330px-African_Bush_Elephant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elephant"},{"title":"Room","blocks":[{"text":"In a building or a ship, a room is any enclosed space within a structure, typically bounded by walls to which entry is possible only via a door or other dividing structure. The entrance connects it to either a passageway, another room, or the outdoors. The space is typically large enough for several people to move about. The size, fixtures, furnishings, and sometimes placement of the room within the building or ship support the activity to be conducted in it."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/18/Ateneul_Rom%C3%A2n_-_Sala.jpg/330px-Ateneul_Rom%C3%A2n_-_Sala.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Room"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png/500px-Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png/500px-Loxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALoxodonta_Elephas_distribution.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Deinotherium12.jpg/500px-Deinotherium12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Deinotherium12.jpg/1280px-Deinotherium12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Deinotherium12.jpg/500px-Deinotherium12.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADeinotherium12.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Gomphotherium_NT_small.jpg/500px-Gomphotherium_NT_small.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Gomphotherium_NT_small.jpg/500px-Gomphotherium_NT_small.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGomphotherium_NT_small.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/African_elephant_%28PSF%29.png/500px-African_elephant_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/African_elephant_%28PSF%29.png/1280px-African_elephant_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4f/African_elephant_%28PSF%29.png/500px-African_elephant_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAfrican_elephant_(PSF).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bb/Indian_elep